userStories.description:
**Goal** — Let a host-enabled Bash call return a task id immediately while the existing launch path keeps running.

**Motivation** — The user wants to keep chatting while it runs and be told when it ends (user, 2026-10-29).

**Approach** — Background commands run **inside the nax chat process**, through the same `Bash` tool path as a foreground command: command policy, approval card, OS sandbox, interceptor rewrite. Detached commands that outlive the chat were rejected because they cannot be sandboxed.
`src/tools/bash.ts`: `BashToolOptions` gains `readonly background?: BackgroundCommandPort`.
- Absent: schema, description and behaviour exactly as today.
- Present: `bashToolInputSchema` adds `runInBackground: { type: "boolean" }`; the description appends one sentence saying the call returns at once with a task id and a notice arrives when the command ends. Use this exact appended sentence: `Set "runInBackground": true to return at once with a task id; a notice arrives when the command ends.`
- `runInBackground: true`: the tool starts `launchIntercepted` without awaiting it, with a tool-owned `AbortController` (never `ctx.signal`) and an already-aborted `releaseDeadline`; adopts the handle with `origin: "agent"`; forwards every `onOutput` chunk to the sink; returns `{ content: "started in the background as <id>", isError: false }`. If the launch promise rejects, `ended` resolves `{ exitCode: -1, timedOut: false, aborted: false, launchError: <message> }` — never an unhandled rejection.
- The interceptor's `postProcess` is not applied to background output.
- New logic lives in `bash-background.ts` helpers so `createBashTool.run` stays within its `check:complexity` baseline.
`nax-tasks__stop` needs no approval card: it only stops a command this chat started. Stopping is immediate: the process group is SIGKILLed, as a turn cancel does today.

**Scope** — In: new port contracts/helpers, optional Bash schema/description, explicit background execution, streaming sink and launch-failure settlement. Out: foreground movement/replay belongs to US-005; session port threading and public exports to US-006; registry/provider to US-007..009. Background commands that survive quitting `nax chat`: rejected by the user, because detached commands cannot run in the OS sandbox. Background support for the `RunCommand` tool's argv branch: only `Bash` gains `runInBackground`. Interceptor `postProcess` rewriting of background command output. Create bash-background.test.ts using _bashToolDeps.runArgv; preserve existing Bash tests with no background option.

**Interface** — New `src/tools/bash-background.ts`:
```ts
export type BackgroundStream = "stdout" | "stderr";
export interface BackgroundCommandEnd {
  readonly exitCode: number;     // -1 when the launch itself failed
  readonly timedOut: boolean;
  readonly aborted: boolean;
  /** Set when the launch rejected (sandbox wrap failure, interceptor throw, spawn failure). */
  readonly launchError?: string;
}
export interface BackgroundCommandHandle {
  readonly command: string;
  readonly description?: string;
  readonly origin: "agent" | "moved";
  readonly ended: Promise<BackgroundCommandEnd>;   // never rejects
  /** Immediately SIGKILLs the process group; `ended` then resolves with `aborted: true`. */
  stop(): void;
}
export interface BackgroundCommandSink {
  readonly id: string;
  append(stream: BackgroundStream, chunk: Uint8Array): void;
}
export interface ForegroundCommand {
  readonly command: string;
  moveToBackground(): void;
}
export interface BackgroundCommandPort {
  adopt(handle: BackgroundCommandHandle): BackgroundCommandSink;
  /** A foreground Bash call registers while it runs; the returned function unregisters it. */
  foreground(call: ForegroundCommand): () => void;
}
```

**Goal** — Track adopted command handles and bounded machine-local logs independently of session lifetime.

**Motivation** — The user wants to keep chatting while it runs and be told when it ends (user, 2026-10-29).

**Approach** — New `src/chat/background/` — `task-types.ts`, `task-registry.ts` and `index.ts`.
State from `BackgroundCommandEnd`: `aborted` → `stopped`; `launchError` → `failed` with `note`; `exitCode === 0` and not `timedOut` → `succeeded`; otherwise `failed`.
When a task ends by itself, the log gets a final line `[nax] exit code <n>` (or `[nax] launch failed: <launchError>`).
`_backgroundTaskDeps` exposes `randomHex`, `mkdir`, `appendLog`, `readLog` for tests.
Resource caps, named constants beside the code that uses them: `TASK_LOG_MAX_BYTES` (50 MiB, after which one line `[nax] log limit reached; further output dropped` is written and later chunks are dropped), `TASK_OUTPUT_MAX_LINES` (500, the most lines `output` returns).
Log append fails → Task keeps running and its state still updates; one `logger.warn`; later chunks dropped. Log reaches `TASK_LOG_MAX_BYTES` → One limit line written; later chunks dropped; task keeps running.
Background tasks belong to the chat host, not one session: `/clear`, `/resume`, ctrl+t, shift+tab and `/model` reopen the session and leave tasks running; notices go to the current session.

**Scope** — In: record types/interface, id/path generation, adoption, ordered writes, byte cap/error isolation, state mapping, newest-first list/get/running and bounded tail output. Out: notice consumption, stop/stopAll, foreground tracking and change events are completed in US-008; provider/prompt composition in US-009; host/session lifecycle in US-010. Persisting background task records across chat processes: tasks are in-memory; only logs stay on disk. Pruning old task log files under `<outputDir>/chat-tasks/`, which, like nax job logs, are machine-local and kept. A cap on the number of concurrently running background commands. Create task-registry.test.ts; use dependency seams and controlled handles rather than spawn a detached process. The full target interface below spans this story and US-008.

**Interface** —
```ts
export type BackgroundTaskState = "running" | "succeeded" | "failed" | "stopped";
export interface BackgroundTask {
  readonly id: string;               // `bash-<8 lowercase hex>`
  readonly command: string;
  readonly description?: string;
  readonly origin: "agent" | "moved";
  readonly startedAt: string;
  readonly endedAt?: string;
  readonly state: BackgroundTaskState;
  readonly exitCode?: number;
  readonly note?: string;            // launchError, when the launch failed
  readonly logPath: string;          // <outputDir>/chat-tasks/<id>.log, machine-local
}
export const TASK_ID_PATTERN = /^bash-[0-9a-f]{8}$/;
export interface ChatBackgroundTasks extends BackgroundCommandPort {
  list(): readonly BackgroundTask[];                 // newest first
  get(id: string): BackgroundTask | undefined;
  running(): readonly BackgroundTask[];
  output(id: string, lines?: number): Promise<string>; // last `lines` log lines, default 40
  stop(id: string): BackgroundTask;                  // NaxError CHAT_TASK_NOT_RUNNING when not running
  stopAll(): Promise<void>;                          // stops every running task; resolves when all ended
  moveForeground(): boolean;                         // newest registered foreground call; false when none
  hasForeground(): boolean;
  peekNotice(): string | undefined;                  // one coalesced notice for every un-noticed ended task
  takeNotice(): string | undefined;                  // returns the peeked text; clears exactly those tasks
  onChange(listener: () => void): () => void;        // adopt, end, foreground register/unregister
}
export function createBackgroundTasks(options: { outputDir: string; now: () => Date; logger: Pick<Logger, "warn"> }): ChatBackgroundTasks;
```
